PRIMARY OBJECTIVE
Guide me through discovery and produce a maintainable project documentation set covering:
Product vision and scope
Functional and non-functional requirements
User journeys and acceptance criteria
System architecture
Data models and external interfaces
Architecture and product decisions
Risks, assumptions, constraints, and unresolved questions
Work Unit organization
Progress and status tracking
Testing and validation strategy
Deployment and operational considerations
Documentation maintenance rules
The final documentation must be precise enough that another AI can implement the project with minimal hidden context.
WORKING METHOD
Use the following stages.
Stage 1: Discovery
Begin by summarizing your current understanding of the concept.
Then identify:
Goals
Intended users
Core user experience
Essential capabilities
Nice-to-have capabilities
Constraints
Assumptions
Risks
Contradictions
Missing information
Ask focused questions in small batches, prioritizing questions that could significantly change the scope or architecture.
Do not ask me to make every technical decision. When appropriate:
Present a recommended default.
Explain the main tradeoff briefly.
Ask me to confirm only if the decision is consequential or difficult to reverse.
Do not silently invent product requirements. Clearly label inferred requirements as assumptions and request confirmation when needed.
Stage 2: Project Definition
Once enough information is available, create the documentation structure listed below.
For each document:
Use clear Markdown.
Add a status: Draft, Proposed, Approved, Superseded, or Archived.
Add a “Last updated” field.
Identify unresolved questions.
Use stable IDs for requirements, decisions, risks, and Work Units.
Link related items by ID.
Avoid duplicating authoritative information across documents; link to the source instead.
Stage 3: Consistency Review
Before declaring the project implementation-ready, check that:
Every product goal is supported by one or more requirements.
Every requirement has acceptance criteria.
Every requirement is assigned to one or more Work Units.
Every Work Unit references the requirements it implements.
Architecture components support identified requirements.
Significant technical choices have decision records.
Risks have mitigations or explicit acceptance.
No Work Unit depends on an undefined interface or unresolved blocking decision.
Testing covers critical requirements and failure modes.
Scope exclusions are explicit.
Terms are used consistently.
Report inconsistencies, gaps, and blockers instead of concealing them.
Stage 4: Implementation Handoff
Prepare the documents so another AI can:
Select the next eligible Work Unit.
Understand its context and dependencies.
Implement only the approved scope.
Run or create appropriate tests.
Record important decisions.
Update progress.
Flag documentation that became inaccurate.
Complete the Work Unit without relying on conversation history.
Stage 5: Living Documentation
Whenever implementation changes the project:
Identify which documents are affected.
Update them in the same change.
Record consequential decisions in the decision log.
Update requirement and Work Unit statuses.
Add newly discovered risks or assumptions.
Do not rewrite historical decisions; supersede them with a new decision.
Keep links and IDs stable.
Record deviations from the approved plan.
Distinguish actual implementation state from intended design.
Provide a concise documentation-change summary.
